codemap 9
tour feature-settings
kind flow
author ai
group features/gui
note The human, reading in the window for a long session on a screen, in a room or with eyes the fixed look does not suit, or who reads call trees top to bottom. Goal: understanding an unfamiliar codebase or a change you did not write takes less friction through codemap than through an editor with an LSP; that editor already shows code in the reader's font, size and colours. Before this feature codemap showed every user Hack at 14 px times the display scale (theme.rs BASE_FONT) on one dark palette, with no key, file or variable to change either; ctrl+wheel zooms only the graph canvas. The graph's direction was session state: a run that pressed graph-turn showed 'left to right' on the button (the tree turned), and the next launch on the same layout file showed 'top to bottom' again, so a reader who prefers trees growing down turns every session. The saved layout file keeps panels only: it is a panel tree rewritten after every drag, so the look is a second file in the same folder, found by the same lookup in io-config, and read before the first frame. The owner asked for exactly these four (font family, font size, default graph layout, theme). Three designs were built and run. A Settings view, a tab like Graph: ctrl+, put it in the centre panel, hiding the code whose font and size were being changed; the tab then stayed in the saved layout, and it broke the rule that every view sits in the default tree. Settings as palette actions ('theme: light', 'font: Menlo', ctrl+= and ctrl+-): no new surface, but nothing on screen says settings exist, the palette closes after each pick so comparing fonts means reopening it, and its selected first row read as the value in force. The kept design: a box over the window, opened by the gear at the right of the top bar, by ctrl+, or by the palette's 'settings'. The reading stays visible behind it, so each change is judged in place, and it stays open for several changes; escape, ctrl+, again, 'close' or a click outside closes it, and while it is open clicks and the wheel reach only the box. Every choice applies on the next frame and is kept per user in ~/.config/codemap/settings (io-config), never in .codemap/; a script or screenshot run reads and writes only the file CODEMAP_SETTINGS names. Theme: dark or light; the renderer repaints every colour through the light palette, so the views keep one set of colour names. Font: Hack (bundled) or any installed monospaced family, which io-fonts lists on a background thread the first time the box opens. Font size: 8 to 32 px at scale 1, for every text in the window. Graph starts: left to right or top to bottom; graph-turn still turns the tree for the session. A run: ctrl+, opens the box; light, larger twice, top to bottom and Menlo redraw the window at once; a second launch on the same file opens light, in Menlo at 16 px, with the graph growing down.

